---
/**
 * /doors — THE DOORS, and the whole point is that a reader does not stay here.
 *
 * A DOOR IS FOR PASSAGE (Huayin, 2026-08-27). attention → reading → curiosity → deeper
 * exploration. A reader encounters a topic that is interesting on its own, chooses to read it, and
 * comes away wanting to explore further. Once the Door has done that, the reader moves beyond it.
 * The durable destinations are elsewhere — Foundations, Analytical Governance, Ask, Columna, the
 * publications. This surface is not one of them and must never grow into one.
 *
 * SO: NOT a publication catalog, NOT a knowledge hub, NOT a taxonomy, NOT a recommendation engine,
 * NOT a metadata-heavy archive, and NOT somewhere anyone is expected to return to. A Door should not
 * become more elaborate than the room beyond it. If this file ever acquires cards, filters,
 * categories, DOI blocks, provenance summaries, reading times, counts or "related" machinery, the
 * surface has failed and the additions are the failure.
 *
 * DOOR IS A ROLE, NOT AN ARTIFACT TYPE. It is not a publication type, an authority class, a
 * provenance class, a standing, or a corpus layer, and it does not require the artifact to be
 * authored onsite or to carry a DOI. Which is why the list lives HERE, in the surface that renders
 * it, and NOT in registry/sources/sources.json:
 *
 *   · that catalog is read by Ask as well as by the website, and corpus membership is keyed on its
 *     `route` field (services/ask/ask/index_build.py). A `route` entry for /doors would silently
 *     enlist an editorial discovery page as an authority-bearing source for the agent.
 *   · `route` and `readingRoute` already carry a load-bearing distinction (src/data/sources.ts):
 *     `route` = a page THIS SITE AUTHORED about a source; `readingRoute` = the work itself. A Door
 *     is neither. It is the editorial role a piece plays in a reader's journey.
 *
 * Ruled explicitly: no `door` flag, no Door authority class, no Door corpus layer, no Door source
 * type. /doors stays inert to Ask corpus membership unless that is ruled otherwise later.
 *
 * SELECTION IS NOT SYMMETRY. Three excellent Doors beat six chosen to fill a shelf. There is no one
 * per territory, no one per framework, no one per publication category. A piece earns Door status by
 * attracting and opening — nothing else.
 *
 * THE HREFS ARE NOT INVENTED HERE. Each INTERNAL destination is the route the registry already
 * records for that work (`route ?? readingRoute` in registry/sources/sources.json). Typed here
 * because the list is four items and a resolver would be more machinery than the surface is allowed;
 * checked against the registry at authoring time, and check_fragments.mjs fails the build if a route
 * stops existing. The one EXTERNAL destination is a canonical URL supplied by Huayin and is not in
 * the registry by ruling — nothing in the build can verify it, so it is verified by the human who
 * published it.
 *
 * TITLES ARE THE ARTIFACTS' OWN. Door discoverability does not authorize retitling: no short
 * editorial label may masquerade as a publication title. The invitation is the editorial voice, and
 * it is visibly a separate sentence beneath the title, never part of it.
 *
 * THE YES MACHINE PROBLEM IS HERE, AND IT IS EXTERNAL (published 2026-08-27; URL supplied by
 * Huayin). The article is LinkedIn-first and LinkedIn is its publication home. The crossing is
 * marked with the site's existing external convention — `rel="noopener" target="_blank"` + a
 * trailing ↗, the /learn pattern — because a reader deserves to know they are leaving.
 *
 * WHAT THIS DOOR IS NOT, by ruling: no onsite duplicate, no /read page, no publication
 * registration, no DOI, no deposit, no Core membership, no Ask ingestion, no new artifact type.
 * A repo editorial draft exists at editorial/drafts/the_yes_machine_problem_v2.md and is the
 * INTERNAL working record only; neither artifact constitutes the other. If someone later proposes
 * mirroring this onsite "for uniformity", that is the ruling being reversed, not a tidy-up.
 *
 * AND IT PROVES THE POINT OF THE SURFACE: a Door is an editorial ROLE, not an artifact type. An
 * external article carries the role exactly as an onsite paper does, which is precisely why Door
 * status never went into registry/sources/sources.json.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// GENERAL DOORS, then the specialist. The order IS the treatment (see `specialist` below): the
// ruling asks for the narrower piece to be present without artificial equal prominence, and to carry
// that with sequencing and prose rather than a category heading or visual machinery.
const DOORS = [
  {
    title: 'Data Has Its Own Ontology',
    invitation: 'Data describes the world. But does data itself have an ontology?',
    href: '/read/data-has-its-own-ontology',
  },
  {
    title: 'Never Let Your Agent Touch the Database',
    invitation: 'An agent can interpret and propose. Should it also hold execution authority?',
    href: '/positions/never-let-your-agent-touch-the-database',
  },
  {
    // THE EXTERNAL DOOR. Third, not last: it is a GENERAL door like the two above it, and the
    // ordering rule here has only ever been general-then-specialist. Its being external is a
    // property of where the work lives, not of how broad it is, and must not push it to the end.
    title: 'The Yes Machine Problem',
    invitation:
      'There used to be people between a question and the database, and they knew when to say no. What carries that judgment now?',
    href: 'https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/yes-machine-problem-huayin-wang-6zzge/',
    external: true,
  },
  {
    title: 'The Two Jobs of the Conditioning Bar',
    invitation:
      'A familiar statistical symbol may be doing two different jobs — and leaving part of inference unrecorded.',
    href: '/read/conditioning-bar',
    // NOT a category, NOT a heading, NOT a badge. One boolean that makes the entry quieter, because
    // the alternative — a second section — would create the taxonomy the ruling forbids.
    specialist: true,
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Doors — datumwise"
  description="A few pieces worth reading, and where each one opens. Start wherever something catches you."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Doors</p>
    <h1 class="serif">Doors</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A few pieces worth reading. Start wherever something catches you — each one opens onto
      something larger.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul class="doors-list">
    {DOORS.map((d) => (
      <li class={d.specialist ? 'door door-specialist' : 'door'}>
        <a
          class="d-title"
          href={d.href}
          {...(d.external ? { rel: 'noopener', target: '_blank' } : {})}
        >{d.title}{d.external ? ' \u2197' : ''}</a>
        <p class="d-invitation">{d.invitation}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  {/* The one line of prose that carries what a heading would otherwise have to say. It names the
      narrower piece as narrower, in the reader's terms, without inventing a category for it. */}
  <p class="tail wayfinding">
    The last one is narrower than the other three — it assumes some statistical familiarity, and
    rewards it.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { width: min(100% - 3rem, 46rem); margin: 0 auto; }
  .page-head h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.6rem); margin: 0.3rem 0 0.8rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 34rem; }

  /* The /positions index list, with the stamp removed and the pull renamed. Hairline separators,
     no cards, no fills — a Door is a title and a reason to cross, and nothing else fits. */
  .doors-list {
    width: min(100% - 3rem, 46rem); margin: 2.6rem auto 0;
    list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 2.2rem;
  }
  .door { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; padding-top: 1.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .d-title { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--rule-strong); width: fit-content; }
  .d-title:hover { text-decoration-color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .d-invitation { margin: 0.35rem 0 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 34rem; }

  /* The specialist treatment, entire: one step quieter in title size and weight. No badge, no
     heading, no rule, no colour — nothing that would read as a category. */
  .door-specialist .d-title { font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 500; }
  .door-specialist .d-invitation { font-size: 0.98rem; }

  .tail { width: min(100% - 3rem, 46rem); margin: 1.6rem auto 0; font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint); }
</style>
